# Lesson Materials

#### Students will use various resources throughout the Thinking Critically About AI unit. It is up to you whether you would like to **print** these materials, or have students access them **virtually**.

<p style="font-size: 18px; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #048899; margin: 20px 0px"><b>⚠️ NOTE</b>: It is essential that students keep all materials for the duration of the unit, as each milestone builds on previous work and materials will be revisited and revised.
</p>

## Know-Want to Know-Learned (KWL) Chart

<img 
  src="https://images.code.org/8dfea09f13634e07750e3de9e4feb6ff-image-1773163148892.17.33 AM.png" 
  alt="" 
  style="width: 30%; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0px 0px 10px 10px; float: right;">

#### Throughout the unit, the KWL chart serves as a living inquiry log and artifact of students' evolving understanding of AI. They will revisit their KWL charts routinely to document their thinking as they progress toward the final project.

#### You may choose to have the KWL chart be **individual**, or use an anchor chart and complete it **as a class**.

### **Implementation Tips:**

* #### **Move Beyond "Filling it Out"**: KWL charts often fail when they are treated as an empty formality. Use the chart to make thinking visible by revisiting it at the start and end of every lesson.
* #### **Focus on Agency**: Use the "W" column to empower students. If a student finds a red flag regarding privacy, that discovery should immediately be turned into a new "Want to Know" inquiry about data security.
* #### **Evidence over Facts**: In the "L" column, prioritize evidence. Instead of writing "AI can be biased," students should record the specific result of an AI testing they conducted in their explorations.

<!-- Sustained Inquiry -->
